From: i7m7r8 <i7m7r8@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH 012] dma-buf: answer the sync ioctl Android userspace sends

Every vendor buffer mapping in this ROM goes through one ioctl number that
mainline spells differently.

Measured:
- libdmabufheap.so BufferAllocator::DoSync (+0x9c40..0x9c74) and
  CpuSyncStart (+0x9cec) build the request as "mov w1, #0x6200" then
  "movk w1, #0x4008, lsl #16" - 0x40086200, which is _IOR('b', 0, struct
  dma_buf_sync) - and retry on EINTR, which is the documented dma-buf
  contract. The same 0x40086200 appears in libgralloccore
  (+0x155d8), libminkdescriptor (+0x9b90) and libQSEEComAPI (+0xaacc,
  +0xac60).
- The stock kernel accepts it: an ioctl-shaped compare in the vendor GKI
  Image tests w1 against 0x40086200 at 0x982c44.
- Upstream include/uapi/linux/dma-buf.h:172 picks _IOW for the same
  number, so dma_buf_ioctl()'s switch (drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c:551)
  would fall through to -ENOIOCTLCMD and userspace would see ENOTTY on
  every begin/end cpu access - which is how a HAL that maps a heap buffer
  loses its mapping, not a cosmetic warning.

The direction bit is not part of the contract; the kernel only ever reads
sync.flags. So match the Android spelling as an alias of the same case
rather than inventing a translation layer, and let both spellings through.
compat_ioctl is compat_ptr_ioctl(), which passes the command number
through unchanged, so the 32-bit HALs are covered by the same label.

---
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c
@@ -537,6 +537,22 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * Android's uapi headers spell DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC with the read direction
+ * bit, _IOR('b', 0, struct dma_buf_sync) = 0x40086200, and vendor userspace
+ * is built against them: BufferAllocator::DoSync (libdmabufheap.so +0x9c64)
+ * loads exactly that value with "mov w1, #0x6200; movk w1, #0x4008, lsl #16"
+ * before calling ioctl(), and snapalloc, gralloc, the camera and audio HALs
+ * and the QSEECom clients all funnel through it. The stock kernel agrees -
+ * its dma_buf_ioctl compares w1 against 0x40086200 (stock Image 0x982c44) -
+ * while upstream chose _IOW. The kernel never looks at the direction bit,
+ * only at the flags inside the struct, so answer both spellings instead of
+ * replying ENOTTY to a well-formed sync and breaking every CPU-side access
+ * to a heap buffer.
+ */
+#define DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC_ANDROID \
+	_IOR(DMA_BUF_BASE, 0, struct dma_buf_sync)
+
 static long dma_buf_ioctl(struct file *file,
 			  unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg)
 {
@@ -549,6 +565,7 @@
 
 	switch (cmd) {
 	case DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC:
+	case DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC_ANDROID:
 		if (copy_from_user(&sync, (void __user *) arg, sizeof(sync)))
 			return -EFAULT;
 
